from ./Resolution import Resolution
from ./Normalization import Normalization
from ./Patterns import Patterns
from ./Conditions import Conditions
from ./Facts import Facts
from ./Strengths import Strengths
from ./DerivationStates import DerivationStates
from ./Relationships import Relationships
from ./Derivation import Derivation
from ./Provenance import Provenance

export anchor Meaning:
    description:
        The previous chapter found the parts of each statement: what it is
        about, what it says, and under what condition. This chapter turns
        those parts into facts, and it is where analysis decides what the
        specification means.

        It does so in steps, each of which can stop short. A name that cannot
        be resolved, a wording no rule reads, a condition that could be a
        state or an event: each is recorded as exactly that, and the steps
        after it work with whatever was understood.

    steps:
        ```text
        resolve      link names in the statement to things the spec defines
        normalize    rewrite known alternative wordings into ones patterns read
        match        recognize the statement's pattern, and produce a fact
        condition    attach the conditions the fact holds under
        derive       add the facts that follow from the facts already found
        ```

        The steps are described in @{Resolution}, @{Normalization},
        @{Patterns}, @{Conditions} and @{Derivation}. What a fact is, and the
        parts it is made of, is described in @{Facts}, with its strength in
        @{Strengths} and how it came to be known in @{DerivationStates}. The
        links between things are described in @{Relationships}, and where
        every result came from in @{Provenance}.

    orderOfReading:
        Facts is the best place to start, because every other section in this
        chapter produces or describes a part of a fact.
